[
  {
    "id": "01_theory_what_is_rlhf",
    "narration": "Let's start by defining Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback, or RLHF. Imagine we've trained a language model on vast amounts of internet text. While it's great at predicting the next word, that doesn't guarantee it will follow instructions helpfully or avoid producing harmful outputs. RLHF is a method for aligning a model's behavior with human preferences, using feedback from people rather than just maximizing likelihood.",
    "focusTarget": "#theory-what-is-rlhf"
  },
  {
    "id": "02_theory_core_idea",
    "narration": "Here's the core idea behind RLHF, captured in two equations. Traditionally, language models maximize the likelihood of the next token given the context. In RLHF, we instead maximize the expected reward for a response, minus a penalty term involving beta times the Kullback-Leibler divergence, or KL divergence, between our model and a reference. Over this lecture, we'll unpack every symbol here—R, beta, KL, pi, and pi_ref—and see why each one matters.",
    "focusTarget": "#theory-core-idea"
  },
  {
    "id": "03_theory_three_stages",
    "narration": "RLHF training happens in three main stages. First, we use Supervised Fine-Tuning, often called SFT, to teach the model to follow instructions using labeled data. Next, we train a reward model that learns to score outputs based on human preferences. Finally, we use Proximal Policy Optimization, or PPO, to fine-tune the model with reinforcement learning, optimizing for high reward while staying close to the original model.",
    "focusTarget": "#theory-three-stages"
  },
  {
    "id": "04_theory_why_not_supervised",
    "narration": "You might wonder, why not just collect more supervised data with perfect answers? In reality, it's much easier for humans to compare two responses and say which is better than to write the ideal answer from scratch. This is why RLHF relies on preference comparisons rather than direct supervision.",
    "focusTarget": "#theory-why-not-supervised"
  },
  {
    "id": "05_theory_pipeline_transition",
