  {
    "id": "01_intro",
    "narration": "Welcome to the world of approximation algorithms! Today, we embark on a journey to tackle some of the most challenging problems in computer science—problems so complex that finding the perfect answer quickly is often impossible. Instead, we’ll learn how to design algorithms that find solutions close to the best, and do so efficiently. This approach is not just a compromise—it's a powerful strategy that enables us to solve real-world problems that would otherwise be out of reach.",
    "focusTarget": "#introBody > div:first-child"
  },
  {
    "id": "02_why_approximation",
    "narration": "Why do we need approximation algorithms? Many important problems—like finding the smallest set of network nodes to cover all connections, or the shortest route visiting every city—are NP-hard. This means that, as the problem size grows, finding the exact solution becomes computationally infeasible. Approximation algorithms give us a way to get answers that are guaranteed to be close to optimal, but in a fraction of the time.",
    "focusTarget": "#sec1"
  },
  {
    "id": "03_key_terminology",
    "narration": "Let's clarify two crucial terms: polynomial time and NP-hardness. An algorithm runs in polynomial time if its steps grow at most as a polynomial function of the input size—think O(n^2) or O(n^3). NP-hard problems, on the other hand, are so tough that no polynomial-time solution is known. For these, approximation algorithms are our best hope for practical solutions.",
    "focusTarget": "#sec1 + .key-concept"
  },
  {
    "id": "04_classic_nphard_examples",
    "narration": "Some classic NP-hard problems include Vertex Cover—finding the smallest set of vertices that touches every edge in a graph—and the Traveling Salesman Problem, where the goal is to find the shortest route visiting every city exactly once. These problems are not just theoretical; they appear in network design, logistics, and many other fields.",
    "focusTarget": "#sec1 + .key-concept + p + .example-box"
  },
  {
    "id": "05_strategies_for_nphard",
    "narration": "When facing NP-hard problems, we have three main strategies. First, exact algorithms guarantee the perfect answer, but often require exponential time. Second, heuristics are fast and can work well in practice, but offer no guarantees. Third, and most importantly for us, approximation algorithms strike a balance: they run quickly and provide provable guarantees on how close their solutions are to optimal.",
    "focusTarget": "#sec1 + .key-concept + p + .example-box + .key-concept + p + .example-box"
  },
  {
    "id": "06_same_problem_diff_answer",
    "narration": "It's vital to understand that approximation algorithms do not change the problem itself. The question remains the same—find the smallest vertex cover, or the shortest tour. What changes is our willingness to accept a solution that's close to optimal, rather than insisting on perfection.",
    "focusTarget": "#sec1 + .key-concept + p + .example-box + .key-concept + p + .example-box + .key-concept"
  },
  {
    "id": "07_common_misconception",
    "narration": "A common misconception is that approximation algorithms give 'wrong' answers. In reality, a 2-approximation for vertex cover, for example, always produces a valid cover—it just might use up to twice as many vertices as the smallest possible cover. The solution is correct, just not always optimal.",
    "focusTarget": "#sec1 + .key-concept + p + .example-box + .key-concept + p + .example-box + .key-concept + .warning-box"
  },
  {
    "id": "08_real_world_applications",
    "narration": "Approximation algorithms are not just academic curiosities—they power real-world systems. For example, network design often relies on finding minimum-cost ways to connect all nodes, which is closely related to the Traveling Salesman and Steiner tree problems. These algorithms make large-scale logistics and communications possible.",
    "focusTarget": "#sec1 + .key-concept + p + .example-box + .key-concept + p + .example-box + .key-concept + .warning-box + .example-box"
  },
  {
    "id": "09_min_vs_max",
    "narration": "Problems come in two flavors: minimization and maximization. In minimization, like vertex cover, we want the smallest possible solution. In maximization, such as finding the largest independent set, we want the biggest. The definition of approximation ratio changes slightly depending on which type we're dealing with.",
    "focusTarget": "#sec2 + p + .example-box"
