    {
      "id": "27_master_theorem_intro",
      "group": "act3_master_theorem",
      "narration": "The Master Theorem is a remarkable shortcut that lets us solve an entire family of recurrences without drawing a single branch of a recursion tree. It applies to any divide-and-conquer recurrence of the form T of n equals a times T of n over b plus O of n to the k, where a tells us how many subproblems we create, b tells us by what factor the input shrinks at each level, and k describes the polynomial work done outside the recursive calls. The theorem reduces the entire analysis to one comparison: is the recursion rate — measured as log base b of a — larger than, equal to, or smaller than the work exponent k? Depending on which wins that competition, we land in one of exactly three cases, each delivering a clean closed-form answer.",
