[
  {
    "id": "01_intro_what_is_recurrence",
    "narration": "Imagine you're searching for a name in a phone book with one thousand pages. Rather than checking every page, you open to the middle, decide which half contains the name, discard the other half, and repeat. After just ten halvings, you're done — that's the power of halving. The question of precisely how many steps that process takes is what a recurrence relation answers. A recurrence relation is an equation that expresses an algorithm's runtime on a problem of size n in terms of its runtime on a smaller input — for our phone book search, that's T of n equals T of n over two, plus one step for the single comparison at each level.",
    "focusTarget": "#intro-what-is"
  },
  {
    "id": "02_intro_why_care",
    "narration": "Here is why this matters: a recurrence relation lets us predict an algorithm's scaling behavior before writing a single line of production code. Solving the relation gives us the Big-O complexity — the mathematical shape of how runtime grows as input size grows. The answers span a dramatic range, from O of log n for efficient divide-and-conquer, all the way to O of 2 to the n for naive recursive approaches. That gap is not subtle — at modest input sizes, it is literally the difference between finishing in milliseconds and never finishing at all. Knowing which category your algorithm falls into, and knowing it analytically rather than empirically, is one of the most powerful skills in computer science.",
    "focusTarget": "#intro-why-care"
  },
  {
    "id": "03_intro_tool_modules",
    "narration": "We have three complementary methods for solving recurrences, and each one offers a different lens on the same problem. The recursion tree method makes the solution visual — we draw every subproblem as a node, every recursive call as a branch, and then read the complexity directly off the structure of the tree. The Master Theorem is a formula shortcut that solves an entire family of divide-and-conquer recurrences almost instantly, once you learn to recognize the pattern. The substitution method is how we prove our answers rigorously, using mathematical induction to confirm that a proposed solution is exactly correct. Think of the three methods as building intuition, applying a shortcut, and then establishing certainty.",
    "focusTarget": "#intro-how-to-use"
  },
  {
    "id": "04_intro_learning_path",
    "narration": "We will follow a five-algorithm progression, carefully ordered from simplest to most revealing. Binary Search sets the baseline — one subproblem per call, a logarithmic answer. Merge Sort introduces branching, where two recursive calls compound the work to O of n log n. Naive Fibonacci is the cautionary tale: overlapping subproblems cause exponential blowup, and the recurrence makes that visible before you ever run the code. Memoized Fibonacci demonstrates how eliminating redundant computation collapses the runtime from exponential to linear in a single conceptual move. Tower of Hanoi closes the progression — an elegantly simple recurrence with an answer that is provably, unavoidably exponential.",
    "focusTarget": "#intro-learning-path"
  },
  {
    "id": "05_intro_complexity_stakes",
    "narration": "Let's make the stakes visceral with concrete numbers. At n equals thirty, O of log n needs about five operations, O of n needs thirty, and O of n log n needs roughly one hundred fifty — all perfectly manageable. But O of 2 to the n needs over one billion operations for that same input size. Push to n equals one hundred, and the exponential algorithm requires a number with thirty digits — more operations than there are atoms in a human body. This is not a rounding error, not a constant factor, not something a faster processor fixes. It is a fundamental difference in algorithmic character, and solving recurrences is the analytical tool that reveals it before you build the wrong thing.",
    "focusTarget": "#intro-complexity"
  },
  {
    "id": "06_intro_collapse_transition",
    "narration": "With that foundation in place, we are ready to build our first recursion tree. The method works by unrolling a recurrence one level at a time — replacing each T of n with the recursive calls it spawns, then doing the same for each of those, until we reach the base cases at the bottom. At that point, we sum the work done at every level to get the total runtime. We will start with Binary Search, the simplest possible case, to build the geometric intuition we will carry through every algorithm that follows.",
    "focusTarget": ".wrap"
  },
  {
    "id": "07_binary_search_preset",
    "narration": "Binary Search achieves its remarkable efficiency through a beautifully simple recurrence relation. At every step, we compare the target against the middle element of a sorted array and discard whichever half cannot contain the answer. This gives us T of n equals T of n over 2, plus a constant — one recursive call on half the input, with constant work at each level. Three parameters fully define this structure: a equals 1 subproblem per call, b equals 2 for the halving divisor, and k equals 0 because the non-recursive work is constant.",
    "focusTarget": "#simSVG"
  },
  {
    "id": "08_binary_search_simulation",
    "narration": "Watching the algorithm execute makes the constant work per level tangible. The middle element is checked — one comparison, constant work — then the search recurses into exactly one of the two halves and discards the other entirely. Playing the full simulation and then stepping through frame by frame reveals the same pattern at every depth: one comparison, one recursive call, one surviving subarray. No matter how large the original input, each individual call does precisely the same fixed amount of work.",
    "focusTarget": "#simSVG"
  },
  {
    "id": "09_binary_search_step_tree",
    "narration": "Now let's build the recursion tree one level at a time to see its geometry directly. Starting from the root representing the full problem of size 16, each step adds exactly one node — first size 8, then 4, then 2. Because a equals 1, there is never a moment of branching: the tree grows straight downward as a single vertical chain. This chain structure is the geometric reason Binary Search is logarithmic — its depth, log base 2 of n, directly equals the total number of comparisons in the worst case.",
    "focusTarget": "#treeSVG"
  },
  {
    "id": "10_binary_search_speed_pause",
    "narration": "Running the animation at a slower pace and pausing mid-way gives us a chance to examine the tree in an intermediate state. Two or three levels have built up so far, each adding exactly one node directly below the previous one. Notice there is no width to speak of — the tree occupies a single column because every recursive call has only one child. We can freeze at any depth and read off the work done so far: it equals the number of levels elapsed, since each contributes exactly one unit.",
    "focusTarget": "#treeSVG"
  },
  {
    "id": "11_binary_search_analyze",
    "narration": "With the tree fully animated and analyzed for n equals 16, the summary confirms our prediction: 4 levels, 4 total nodes, 1 leaf, and a grand total of 4 units of work — exactly log base 2 of 16. The per-level breakdown shows a perfectly flat pattern: 1 unit at every depth, no growth, no accumulation. Summing log n identical contributions gives log n total, which is the definitive proof that Binary Search is O of log n — the skinniest recursion tree possible, where depth equals total work.",
    "focusTarget": "#treeSVG"
  },
  {
    "id": "12_merge_sort_intro",
    "narration": "Merge Sort changes exactly one thing in the recurrence — the branching factor a — and that single change completely transforms the tree. Instead of T of n equals T of n over 2 plus 1, we now have T of n equals 2 times T of n over 2 plus n. The branching factor a jumps from 1 to 2: we sort the left half recursively and the right half recursively, then merge them in O of n time. Every node in this new tree will split into two children rather than one, and the work at each node is proportional to n, not constant, because merging requires scanning all elements.",
    "focusTarget": "#simSVG"
  },
  {
    "id": "13_merge_sort_step_tree",
    "narration": "Stepping through Merge Sort's recursion tree makes the doubling immediate and dramatic. Level 0 holds a single root processing 16 elements, which spawns two children at level 1 — each handling 8 elements — and those two nodes each branch again at level 2, producing four nodes of size 4. At every step, the tree doubles in width because each node makes two recursive calls, and this branching continues all the way down to the base cases. The contrast with Binary Search's single vertical chain could not be sharper.",
    "focusTarget": "#treeSVG"
  },
  {
    "id": "14_merge_sort_full_animation",
    "narration": "The complete Merge Sort tree for n equals 16 is a perfect binary tree with 4 levels, 16 leaf nodes, and 31 total nodes — a stark contrast to Binary Search's chain of just 4 nodes. This exponential growth in node count might look alarming: does Merge Sort do exponentially more work? The answer is no, and the reason is one of the most elegant results in algorithm analysis. Despite the exponential width, the total work contributed at each level stays perfectly constant — and that is exactly the insight we need.",
    "focusTarget": "#treeSVG"
  },
  {
    "id": "15_merge_sort_work_per_level",
    "narration": "Here is the central insight: at every level of the Merge Sort tree, the total work adds up to exactly n. Level 0 has one node merging 16 elements — total work 16. Level 1 has two nodes each merging 8 — still 16 total. Level 2 has four nodes each merging 4 — still 16. The exponential growth in node count is perfectly canceled by the exponential shrinkage in each node's problem size, because the branching factor a equals the divisor b equals 2. With log n levels each contributing exactly n units of work, the grand total is n times log n — and that is the complete, rigorous explanation for why Merge Sort is O of n log n.",
    "focusTarget": "#treeSVG"
  },
  {
    "id": "16_fib_naive_intro",
    "narration": "Fibonacci is perhaps the most famous recursive algorithm, but its naive implementation exposes a fundamental flaw in recursive design. The recurrence T of n equals T of n minus 1, plus T of n minus 2, plus O of 1 — two recursive calls that each only reduce the input by one. Unlike Merge Sort, which halves the problem at every level, both of these calls leave subproblems nearly the same size as the original. We are making two almost-full-sized recursive calls at every single step.",
    "focusTarget": ".wrap"
  },
  {
    "id": "17_fib_naive_duplicates",
    "narration": "The highlighted duplicate nodes reveal the root cause of the problem. Fibonacci of 3 is computed twice, Fibonacci of 2 is computed three times, and that redundancy compounds as we go deeper. Every duplicate is wasted work — computing a value we have already calculated and discarded. The tree does not just grow large, it grows large with repeat entries, and that redundant computation is the direct cause of exponential complexity.",
    "focusTarget": ".wrap"
  },
  {
    "id": "18_fib_naive_explosion",
    "narration": "Watch the tree grow — it expands explosively and asymmetrically. The left branch dives much deeper because T of n minus 1 is always a larger subproblem than T of n minus 2, so the left side of the tree is strictly taller than the right. At each level the node count roughly doubles, which gives us O of 2 to the n total nodes. Even at the small value n equals 7, the tree is already enormous — scale that to n equals 40 and you would need over a billion recursive calls for a sequence a simple loop computes in 40 steps.",
    "focusTarget": ".wrap"
  },
  {
    "id": "19_fib_naive_work_per_level",
    "narration": "Stepping through the tree level by level makes the contrast with Merge Sort visceral. In Merge Sort, every level costs the same total work — O of n — because wider levels have proportionally cheaper subproblems. Here, each level has more nodes and every node does constant work, so the total cost per level doubles each time we descend. Level zero costs 1, level one costs 2, level two costs 4 — a geometric series that sums to 2 to the n.",
    "focusTarget": ".wrap"
  },
  {
    "id": "20_hanoi_intro",
    "narration": "The Tower of Hanoi puzzle gives us another two-call recurrence with barely-shrinking subproblems: T of n equals 2 times T of n minus 1, plus 1. To move n disks, you move the top n minus 1 disks to an auxiliary peg, slide the largest disk directly to the destination, then move those n minus 1 disks again — two recursive calls, each on a problem of size n minus 1. This decomposition is not just one implementation strategy; it is the only valid decomposition, which means the exponential we will find is inherent to the problem.",
    "focusTarget": ".wrap"
  },
  {
    "id": "21_hanoi_animation",
    "narration": "Notice how different this tree looks from Fibonacci's lopsided bush — the Tower of Hanoi tree is perfectly symmetric. Every left subtree mirrors its right sibling exactly, because both recursive calls tackle the identical subproblem size, n minus 1. Level i holds exactly 2 to the i nodes, and summing across all levels gives 2 to the n minus 1 total moves. Four disks need 15 moves, ten disks need 1023, and 64 disks would require over 18 quintillion moves — a number so large that working at one move per second would take roughly 585 billion years.",
    "focusTarget": ".wrap"
  },
  {
    "id": "22_hanoi_fib_comparison",
    "narration": "Both naive Fibonacci and Tower of Hanoi land at O of 2 to the n, but they arrive there for completely different reasons. Fibonacci's exponential is wasteful — we recompute values we already know, and we will soon fix that. Hanoi's exponential is necessary — no algorithm can solve the puzzle with fewer moves, so no optimization can improve the bound. When you encounter an exponential complexity, the first question to ask is: is this inherent to the problem structure, or is it an artifact of a poor implementation?",
    "focusTarget": ".wrap"
  },
  {
    "id": "23_fib_memoized_concept",
    "narration": "Memoization is conceptually simple: before computing Fibonacci of k, check a lookup table of previously computed results. If the answer is already there, return it in O of 1 time. If not, compute it, store it, and return it. Each value is computed exactly once and looked up for free every subsequent time. The mathematical recurrence has not changed — it still says T of n depends on T of n minus 1 and T of n minus 2 — but the execution pattern changes completely.",
    "focusTarget": ".wrap"
  },
  {
    "id": "24_fib_memoized_animation",
    "narration": "The tree has transformed from a bush into a chain. Starting from n, we compute Fibonacci of n minus 1, which computes n minus 2, which computes n minus 3 — a straight line down to the base case. When the second recursive branch T of n minus 2 fires, that value is already in the table and returns immediately as an O of 1 hit. Every branch that caused the explosion in naive Fibonacci has been pruned to a single instant lookup, and the massive tree collapses to a single spine.",
    "focusTarget": ".wrap"
  },
  {
    "id": "25_fib_memoized_complexity",
    "narration": "The complexity is now O of n — we compute exactly n distinct Fibonacci values, each exactly once, each in O of 1 after the first. We went from over a billion operations for n equals 40 to just 40, by storing results we already computed. This is the core insight behind dynamic programming: identify overlapping subproblems, store their solutions, and never compute the same thing twice. Same problem, same recurrence structure — but memoization transforms exponential into linear.",
    "focusTarget": ".wrap"
  },
  {
    "id": "26_act2_transition_master",
    "narration": "We have now seen the full spectrum of recursive complexity: logarithmic from Binary Search, linear-log from Merge Sort, exponential from naive Fibonacci and Tower of Hanoi, and linear from memoized Fibonacci. The recursion tree gives us powerful visual intuition for why these bounds arise, but analyzing divide-and-conquer recurrences by hand every time is tedious. The Master Theorem, which we explore next, provides a unified framework for reading off the answer in seconds.",
    "focusTarget": ".wrap"
  },
  {
    "id": "27_master_theorem_intro",
    "narration": "The Master Theorem is a remarkable shortcut that lets us solve an entire family of recurrences without drawing a single branch of a recursion tree. It applies to any divide-and-conquer recurrence of the form T of n equals a times T of n over b plus O of n to the k, where a tells us how many subproblems we create, b tells us by what factor the input shrinks at each level, and k describes the polynomial work done outside the recursive calls. The theorem reduces the entire analysis to one comparison: is the recursion rate — measured as log base b of a — larger than, equal to, or smaller than the work exponent k? Depending on which wins that competition, we land in one of exactly three cases, each delivering a clean closed-form answer.",
    "focusTarget": ".wrap"
  },
  {
    "id": "28_case2_merge_sort",
    "narration": "Merge Sort is the textbook illustration of Case Two — the perfectly balanced case. With a equals 2 recursive calls each on a problem of size n over 2, we have b equals 2, and the merge step costs O of n, so k equals 1. Computing log base 2 of 2 gives exactly 1, which equals k precisely, placing us in Case Two. When the recursion rate and work exponent are equal, every single level of the recursion tree contributes the same total work, and since there are log n levels, we accumulate exactly one extra logarithmic factor — yielding T of n equals Theta of n log n. This is the algebraic confirmation of what we observed by hand: every level of the Merge Sort tree does exactly n total work.",
    "focusTarget": ".wrap"
  },
  {
    "id": "29_case2_binary_search",
    "narration": "Binary Search lands in Case Two as well, but notice how strikingly minimal the numbers are. We make just one recursive call — a equals 1 — on half the input, giving b equals 2, and the comparison itself is constant time so k equals 0. Log base 2 of 1 equals 0, which equals k, confirming we are squarely in the balanced case once more. The base work of O of 1 accumulates across log n levels, yielding T of n equals Theta of log n — the same logarithmic depth we watched unfold one split at a time in the Binary Search recursion tree.",
    "focusTarget": ".wrap"
  },
  {
    "id": "30_case1_recursion_dominates",
    "narration": "Now let's see what happens when the recursion rate overtakes the work rate. With a equals 4 subproblems each of size n over 2, the recursion rate is log base 2 of 4 which equals 2 — strictly greater than k equals 1. This is Case One: as we descend the tree, we spawn more and more subproblems, and the total work at each successive level grows rather than staying flat. The leaves of the recursion tree completely dominate the total cost, and the theorem gives us T of n equals Theta of n to the log base b of a — in this case Theta of n squared. For a concrete check: level one already has 4 subproblems each doing n over 2 work, contributing 2n total, already more than the root's n.",
    "focusTarget": ".wrap"
  },
  {
    "id": "31_case3_work_dominates",
    "narration": "Case Three flips the balance entirely: now the work exponent k defeats the recursion rate. With a equals 2 and b equals 2, the recursion rate is log base 2 of 2 equals 1, but k equals 2 is strictly larger. As we go deeper in the tree, each subproblem is smaller and the work it contributes shrinks faster than new subproblems can accumulate. The root of the tree does the most work of any single node, and the total cost collapses to just the root's contribution: T of n equals Theta of n to the k, which here gives Theta of n squared.",
    "focusTarget": ".wrap"
  },
  {
    "id": "32_case1_vs_case3_contrast",
    "narration": "Here is a thought-provoking coincidence worth pausing on: Case One with a equals 4 and Case Three with k equals 2 both deliver T of n equals Theta of n squared — the identical asymptotic complexity. Yet the structural reasons are completely opposite: in Case One, the leaves dominate because work multiplies as we descend; in Case Three, the root dominates because work shrinks as we descend. Same final answer, diametrically different internal mechanics. This is precisely why the Master Theorem distinguishes three cases — each describes a fundamentally different computational shape, and knowing which case you are in tells you exactly whether reducing a, increasing b, or reducing k will actually buy you a faster algorithm.",
    "focusTarget": ".wrap"
  },
  {
    "id": "33_master_limitations",
    "narration": "The Master Theorem is powerful, but it enforces a strict boundary: it only works when the input is divided by a factor b. Recurrences where we subtract a constant — like naive Fibonacci with T of n equals T of n minus 1 plus T of n minus 2, or Tower of Hanoi with T of n equals 2 times T of n minus 1 plus 1 — fall completely outside its reach. Both of those recurrences appear in the classic examples table marked as not applicable, because no fixed ratio b exists in their definitions. For those subtractive recurrences, we need a technique that works by making an educated guess and verifying it rigorously through mathematical induction.",
    "focusTarget": ".wrap"
  },
  {
    "id": "34_master_experiment",
    "narration": "The three-parameter space of the Master Theorem opens up a rich landscape worth exploring on your own. Can you find values that produce Theta of n cubed? Try Case Three with k equals 3 — any a and b where log base b of a is less than 3 will do it, such as a equals 2, b equals 2, k equals 3. What about Theta of square root of n? You need log base b of a to equal one half, achievable with a equals 2 and b equals 4, which gives log base 4 of 2 equals 0.5. Developing this fluency — instantly recognizing which case activates and what levers to pull — is what separates algorithmic intuition from mere memorization.",
    "focusTarget": ".wrap"
  },
  {
    "id": "35_transition_to_substitution",
    "narration": "We have now assembled three complementary tools for solving recurrences. The recursion tree built visual intuition by tracing every level of computation. The Master Theorem delivered near-instant answers for divide-and-conquer recurrences through a single rate comparison. But for recurrences that fall outside the Master Theorem's reach — and for those moments when we need a formally airtight proof rather than a pattern match — we turn to the substitution method. The substitution method works by guessing the closed-form complexity, then using strong induction to verify that the guess satisfies the original recurrence exactly.",
    "focusTarget": ".wrap"
  },
  {
    "id": "36_substitution_intro",
    "narration": "The substitution method is formal proof by mathematical induction applied to recurrences. The pattern is always the same five steps: guess the closed-form answer, assume that guess holds for all inputs strictly smaller than n, substitute that assumption into the recurrence, perform algebra to verify consistency, then check the base case. The hard part is the guess — and our recursion tree analysis has already done that work for us. Starting with Binary Search, we will build the intuition for all three stages of the inductive argument before moving on to harder examples.",
    "focusTarget": ".wrap"
  },
  {
    "id": "37_binary_search_recurrence_guess",
    "narration": "Binary Search satisfies T of n equals T of n over 2 plus 1, with base case T of 1 equals 1 — halve the search space, do one comparison. From the recursion tree we already know this runs in log n levels with constant work per level, so our guess is T of n is at most c times log base 2 of n for some constant c we will determine. We are not proving the guess yet — we are only committing to a form that the induction will confirm. The guess is the creative leap; everything that follows is algebra.",
    "focusTarget": ".wrap"
  },
  {
    "id": "38_binary_search_induction",
    "narration": "The inductive hypothesis is the logical engine of the proof: we assume our guess holds for every input strictly smaller than n — specifically, that T of n over 2 is at most c times log base 2 of n over 2. We are allowed to assume this because n over 2 is strictly smaller than n, so strong induction applies. Think of it as borrowing from the future: the proof for size n borrows the already-established result for size n over 2, and the whole chain is anchored by the base case we will verify at the end.",
    "focusTarget": ".wrap"
  },
  {
    "id": "39_binary_search_substitute",
    "narration": "Now we substitute: T of n equals T of n over 2 plus 1, which is at most c times log base 2 of n over 2, plus 1. The logarithm identity log of n over 2 equals log n minus 1 transforms this into c times log n minus c plus 1. We need this entire expression to be at most c times log n — our target bound. Subtracting c log n from both sides, we need negative c plus 1 to be at most zero, which means c must be at least 1. Any constant greater than or equal to 1 closes the induction.",
    "focusTarget": ".wrap"
  },
  {
    "id": "40_binary_search_verify_reflect",
    "narration": "The final step anchors the induction at n equals 2: T of 2 is 2, and c times log base 2 of 2 equals c, so we need c at least 2 — fully consistent with our earlier condition. The proof is complete: Binary Search runs in O of log n time, established by mathematical induction. Notice that only one step required genuine insight — the guess. Every other step was mechanical algebra, which is precisely the power of this method.",
    "focusTarget": ".wrap"
  },
  {
    "id": "41_merge_sort_proof",
    "narration": "With the five-step pattern internalized, we can move through Merge Sort at pace. Its recurrence is T of n equals 2 T of n over 2 plus n, and our guess — motivated by the n log n total work we counted level by level in the tree — is T of n at most c times n times log n. Substituting the hypothesis: 2 times c times n over 2 times log of n over 2 plus n simplifies to c n log n minus cn plus n. We need the leftover terms minus cn plus n to be non-positive, giving c at least 1 — the same threshold as Binary Search. The recursion tree supplied the guess; induction delivers the proof.",
    "focusTarget": ".wrap"
  },
  {
    "id": "42_fibonacci_naive_proof",
    "narration": "Naive Fibonacci breaks the divide-and-conquer mold: its recurrence T of n equals T of n minus 1 plus T of n minus 2 plus 1 subtracts constants rather than dividing, so the Master Theorem does not apply. Substitution still can. We guess T of n at most c times 2 to the n, motivated by the explosive tree we observed earlier. Substituting: c times 2 to the n minus 1 plus c times 2 to the n minus 2 plus 1 equals three-quarters c times 2 to the n plus 1, which is at most c times 2 to the n when c is at least 4. This is a result only substitution can reach — a direct payoff for mastering all three methods.",
    "focusTarget": ".wrap"
  },
  {
    "id": "43_fibonacci_memoized_contrast",
    "narration": "Memoized Fibonacci is the starkest contrast: each of the n distinct subproblems is solved exactly once in constant time, so total work is simply n times O of 1, which is O of n. The inductive argument is almost trivial — the cache guarantees no redundant computation, making the proof reduce to counting n unique calls. Side by side, naive and memoized Fibonacci share the same recurrence structure but differ by one design decision — caching — and the complexity swings from exponential to linear. That is the power of understanding your recurrence deeply enough to choose the right implementation.",
    "focusTarget": ".wrap"
  },
  {
    "id": "44_substitution_synthesis",
    "narration": "The substitution method rewards the analyst who invests in building a recursion tree first — a poor guess leads to algebra that never closes, a direct signal to return and rebuild your intuition. Together our three methods form a complete toolkit: the recursion tree builds geometric intuition by unrolling levels visually; the Master Theorem delivers instant answers for standard divide-and-conquer cases; and substitution provides the rigorous inductive backbone that makes every result bulletproof. With these three tools you can analyze the time complexity of any recursive algorithm from first principles, whether it divides, subtracts, or branches in ways no shortcut formula covers.",
    "focusTarget": ".wrap"
  },
  {
    "id": "45_epilogue_merge_sort_anchor",
    "narration": "We have traveled far in this lecture — from the mechanics of a single recursive call all the way to formal mathematical proof by induction. Let's return to Merge Sort as our closing visual anchor, because it embodies everything we have studied: it divides its input in half at every level and performs linear work across each level, giving us Theta of n log n. What makes Merge Sort perfect for this moment is that all three of our solution methods converge on the same answer from completely different starting points. That convergence — tree, theorem, and proof all agreeing — is the deepest validation that our toolkit is sound.",
    "focusTarget": ".wrap"
  },
  {
    "id": "46_three_methods_synthesis",
    "narration": "Today we learned three distinct ways to solve a recurrence relation, and each one offers something the others cannot. Recursion trees let you visualize the work at every level — you can literally see where the time is being spent and why the cost accumulates the way it does. The Master Theorem gives you a mechanical computation: identify the branching factor a, the shrinkage factor b, and the per-level work exponent k, and the closed-form answer drops out in seconds. The substitution method then turns that answer into a rigorous proof by induction, guaranteeing correctness even in cases where the other two methods cannot reach. Three tools, three perspectives, one truth.",
    "focusTarget": ".wrap"
  },
  {
    "id": "47_when_to_use_each_method",
    "narration": "Knowing which tool to reach for is as important as knowing how to use each one. Reach for the recursion tree whenever you need intuition — when you want to understand WHY an algorithm is fast or slow, not just that it is. Reach for the Master Theorem in an exam, an interview, or a code review when you need a quick, reliable answer and your recurrence fits the divide-and-conquer mold. And reach for the substitution method when the Master Theorem's conditions are not met, or when you need a proof that will survive mathematical scrutiny. These three use cases are complementary, not competitive — the analyst who knows all three is never stuck.",
    "focusTarget": ".wrap"
  },
  {
    "id": "48_memoization_naive_explosion",
    "narration": "If you carry one insight forward from this entire lecture, let it come from the Fibonacci story. In its naive recursive form, computing the nth Fibonacci number recalculates the same subproblems an exponential number of times — the same values are recomputed from scratch at every branch. The resulting tree explodes into a vast, asymmetric structure with O of 2 to the n nodes; at n equals fifty, that is more than one quadrillion operations. This is not an edge case or a quirk — it is the inevitable consequence of redundancy baked into the recursion itself.",
    "focusTarget": ".wrap"
  }
]

